I would use a `HashMap<Character, Integer>` to count the occurrences of each character in the string. I would initialize the HashMap and then iterate through the string once to populate the counts. Afterward, I would iterate through the string again, checking the values in the HashMap to find the first character that has a count of one. 

Here’s a brief implementation outline:

```java
public char findFirstNonRepeatingChar(String str) {
    Map<Character, Integer> charCount = new HashMap<>();

    // First pass: Count occurrences of each character
    for (char c : str.toCharArray()) {
        charCount.put(c, charCount.getOrDefault(c, 0) + 1);
    }

    // Second pass: Find the first non-repeating character
    for (char c : str.toCharArray()) {
        if (charCount.get(c) == 1) {
            return c;
        }
    }

    // If no non-repeating character is found (which should never happen in a valid input), return some default value or throw an exception
    return '\0'; // Or throw a custom exception
}
```

